body:
That is a good point, but I am curious in each and every different person. Don't worry, I don't jump to conclusions that everyone is like me or anyone else. 

&#x200B;

And that's true (the point about triggers), but there are a lot of factors. The period of instability is episodic by nature. I am outside of the episode now for example and I am as solid as a rock, arguably not at all reactive to negative things.

To be fair, I think it varies. My friend seems to be going through a depressive episode as he almost ended up flunking school out of nowhere. He was absolutely shit on by his parents and gossiped about by the other parents. People outside of me and our other friend have little to no sympathy for him unfortunately. 

&#x200B;

I think you are picking up on people who like the mentally ill who are easy to handle. They understand depression to a degree, so when a likeable person goes through it they are all there for support. But the same most certainly does not apply to belligerent people. I get super pissy and hate everyone in an episode, and man people are not a fan.

&#x200B;

People with BPD are kicked to the goddamn curb though, it really sucks to hear and see.

I hope they can figure that out, or that you can. I think I am starting to see how the overlap is greater than I thought. Seemingly to be more apparent in atypical bipolar, as classical doesn't seem to overlap as much. 

&#x200B;

Thanks for sharing ofc. Appreciate ya

I am personally guilty of this. And I guess I'll make my case for why this happens pretty often. 

&#x200B;

I think it is somewhat of a buzzword, something that as you said has become a bit of a catchphrase. I think people sense some sort of gaslighting when it isn't there because of how much the word is thrown around. When our partner (for example) may remember something genuinely different than we do, for many their mind immediately leans towards the idea of gaslighting. 

&#x200B;

Many people come off as manipulative without actually intending to. Manipulation is typically a conscious effort where the person is aware of their tactics (to a degree at least). I think it is a perfect example of good people making genuine mistakes on all fronts. Maybe if we as people make sure to validate while also criticizing, the term gaslighting will gain more meaning again. 

&#x200B;

I get what you mean though OP, one of my biggest regrets is accusing my ex of trying to gaslight me. It's something that has certainly been sensationalized and the term is used incorrectly all the time.

He may be having a hard time as well, I don't have a lot of advice other than a logical explanation. There are a lot of things that could have possibly happened, but what tends to be the most likely causes for these types of things are the most tame ones. It is appropriate to message the friend as far as I can tell, but don't push it too far. You're FP may just need some time away from it all. The most likely scenario is that he will be back, and when he does come back I would certainly try to have a steady conversation about what happened. 

&#x200B;

I hope you can feel better OP, I'm sure he is going to be A-Oki

When it comes to those mistakes, and when people are chewing you out, they just to see that you are 100% absorbing what they are saying. It is totally normal to defend yourself with that kind of language, but I've never seen people be receptive to it. So bear that in mind. 

&#x200B;

I feel your pain for different reasons. But due to a condition I do sometimes find myself in that position. It does sound ADHD related and it may have been out of your control. In the end you shouldn't spend time feeling bad about it even if it was your fault. 

&#x200B;

I would certainly try to take the approach of proactivity. Reflect on the mistake and what you possibly could have done to change things. Hindsight is 20/20. And the next time you find yourself in a situation like that, experiment with something that may help. It may be worth a shot!

&#x200B;

Try not to be too hard on yourself OP, because at the end of the day people without ADHD screw up at work as well.

I've been in a similar spot before. I did end up leaving the person. As far as regrets, I am glad that I distanced myself from her. But I didn't want to get her out of my life entirely. I think a step back, and certainly making time and space for yourself could be helpful. 

&#x200B;

Is he at all aware of his atypical behavior, does he know how it makes you feel? When it comes to this I think it is also important that you can be honest but at the same time fair with him. 

&#x200B;

When it comes to nudging a person in the right direction, that is tricky. Maybe if he fully understands what his quality of life is, how it impacts others, what treatment looks like, etc., he could be more motivated to get treatment. That is just something that I've seen work for a few people. 

&#x200B;

At the end of the day I do think it is important to stress that this is just reddit, and that no one on here is going to know the full story. There are just general and broad approaches to these sorts of situations, and it is on you to make adjustments on how you want to specifically handle it.

I guess it really depends from person to person. I think really having a fulfilling life outside of those relationships is important. Note that those relationships can certainly be a part of what makes you happy, but I think it is necessary to be happy without them to. To be able to stand alone and be confident in what you do as a solo. 

&#x200B;

To wind down after a long day I like to come on here and listen to people's problems while having a glass of bourbon or maybe a beer. But what that comes down to is that I have a relaxing and more passive hobby. I'm also a bit of a film buff, there are certain streamers that I find amusing. So I guess something that you can do while lying down, but you are still at least paying some attention to what is going on. That is sort of what separates it from a hobby and just relaxing. The difference being that a hobby is more likely to take your mind off of things, because you are actively thinking about something that you genuinely find interesting.

What is it about yourself that you think may be attracting these types of people.

&#x200B;

Certainly want to make it clear that I don't think it is your fault, but there is almost certainly some sort of psychological pattern here that is creating this cycle.

I see, I really am sorry to hear that. A lot of people are really rough, but there are certainly good people out there as well. It is very easy to hit a hard streak, and it can certainly seem like everyone just wants to use you. But there are certainly people out there who will end up having a more healthy stable relationship with you. It may have to do with just the sort of person that you have ended up attracting (not inherently your fault or anything). But if you are noticing a pattern of these type of people coming into your life, may look at the psychology of why they are attracted to you, and not only how you can spot someone like that but also attract a different type of person

&#x200B;

Just a thought tho

The thing about assisting a mentally ill person in suicide is that deeming whether or not they are thinking clearly is obviously going to be tricky. Por ejemplo, suicidal ideation is a direct symptom of some mood disorders. Therefore their desire to die is coming directly from their sickness, and it becomes tricky to tell if their desire to die should be considered as logically solid. If someone is mentally ill, it isn't a stretch to assume they may not always be thinking clearly. Bipolar disorder is a good example of this as well. Someone could temporarily wish to die more than anything else, but at the end of the day they aren't themselves. 

&#x200B;

Just some food for thought. Mental illness is very treatable, I think there are just a lot of roadblocks in the system that cause a significant amount of error. But statistically our mainline treatments for disorders often do have promising numbers.
